# pheasant-kb environment: copy to `.env` beside compose.yaml and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose loads `.env` from the directory you run `docker compose` in,
# so these values reach the containers as environment variables without being
# written into a config file, an image or `/state`. `.env` is gitignored; this
# template is not, so it must never hold a real secret.
#
# Only OPENAI_API_KEY is required. Everything else has the measured default
# shown beside it; uncomment a line only to change it. What each part does,
# and when to move its number, is in docs/pheasant-kb/scale.md.
#
# Other deployment shapes (one container, local profiles, the generic fleet)
# read deploy/compose/.env.example instead.

# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED
# =============================================================================
# Embeddings (text-embedding-3-small), image captions, transcripts, the
# assistant (gpt-6-luna) and its evidence judgements.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-replace-me

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Or: every model call on an Azure AI Foundry resource instead of OpenAI —
# OpenAI models deployed there, or Microsoft's own (MAI, Microsoft-Decision-1).
# Uncomment all four, copy deploy/compose/foundry/pheasant-kb.foundry.yaml to
# ./pheasant.yaml and put your resource name in it. Walk-through:
# docs/pheasant-kb/foundry.md. (On Windows, `;` between the COMPOSE_FILE paths.)
# AZURE_FOUNDRY_API_KEY=
# PHEASANT_KB_CONFIG=./pheasant.yaml
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:deploy/compose/foundry/compose.foundry.yaml
# OPENAI_API_KEY=unused     # replaces the line above; no container receives it

# =============================================================================
# ACCESS
# =============================================================================
# Host address and port for the UI, HTTP API and MCP endpoint. Loopback by
# default: a token is authentication, not a perimeter, and Docker's iptables
# rules bypass most host firewalls. Widen only behind an ingress.
# PHEASANT_BIND=127.0.0.1
# PHEASANT_PORT=8765

# The four internal tokens are generated on first boot and kept on the
# `pheasant-secrets` volume. Read the API token (for the UI's "Connect API"
# and for MCP clients) with:
#
#   docker compose exec api sh -c 'cat /run/pheasant/secrets/api-token'
#
# Set one here only to choose or rotate it; it replaces the stored value on the
# next `docker compose up`. They must all differ (`openssl rand -hex 32` each).
# PHEASANT_API_TOKEN=
# PHEASANT_GRAPH_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# PHEASANT_INDEX_WORKER_TOKEN=
# PHEASANT_INGESTION_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Postgres is reachable only on the Compose network. Set a real password if
# you ever publish it. Set before the first boot: the database keeps the
# password it was created with.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pheasant

# Private GitHub repositories added as sources. A fine-grained PAT scoped to
# "Contents: Read-only" on those repositories is the least-privilege choice.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# =============================================================================
# CONFIGURATION AND STORAGE
# =============================================================================
# The pheasant.yaml every tier mounts. Default: the shipped pheasant-kb config.
# Copy it, edit the copy, and point this at it (docs/pheasant-kb/configure.md).
# PHEASANT_KB_CONFIG=./deploy/compose/pheasant-kb.yaml

# Where repositories added by URL are cloned. Default: the named volume
# `pheasant-workspace`. Set a host directory to see or back up the clones.
# PHEASANT_FLEET_WORKSPACE_PATH=./workspace

# Where `docker compose run --rm backup` writes, and `restore` reads.
# PHEASANT_BACKUP_PATH=./backups

# Which image runs. Default: the release this checkout names. A locally built
# image (`docker compose build`) is tagged with this name.
# PHEASANT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/esatt10/pheasant:0.15.1

# Upgrading from the stack formerly called pheasant-lab? Keep its volumes, and
# therefore its index, by keeping its project name:
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=pheasant-lab

# =============================================================================
# SIZE — scale each part up or down (limits, not reservations)
# =============================================================================
# api: the UI, HTTP and MCP. Holds result sets and the resident vector matrix
# (4 bytes x 1,536 per chunk: ~600 MB at 100k chunks), no graph.
# PHEASANT_API_MEMORY=4096m
# PHEASANT_API_CPUS=2.0

# graph: answers graph queries for the api tier.
# PHEASANT_GRAPH_MEMORY=2560m
# PHEASANT_GRAPH_CPUS=1.5

# indexer: the one writer. Its memory grows with the corpus (graph working set)
# and peaks at the end of a sync, when vector maintenance runs.
# PHEASANT_INDEXER_MEMORY=6144m
# PHEASANT_INDEXER_CPUS=2.0

# worker: parse/extract/chunk. Ingest throughput scales with these three.
# PHEASANT_WORKERS=4
# PHEASANT_WORKER_THREADS=2
# PHEASANT_WORKER_MEMORY=512m
# PHEASANT_WORKER_CPUS=1.0

# logger: drains the observation queue.
# PHEASANT_LOGGER_MEMORY=1024m
# PHEASANT_LOGGER_CPUS=1.0

# postgres: state, chunks and the BM25 index.
# PHEASANT_POSTGRES_MEMORY=1280m
# PHEASANT_POSTGRES_CPUS=2.0
# PHEASANT_POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS=100

# nats: the index and observation queues.
# PHEASANT_NATS_MEMORY=256m
# PHEASANT_NATS_CPUS=0.5
